    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS with the acceptance test green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI agree with the finding \u2014 the dialling-code set and the longest-prefix selection were duplicated across the create and read paths. I gave both a single owner.\n\n**New `src/main/java/.../util/E164PhoneNumbers.java`** \u2014 a small final utility class holding:\n- `NATIONAL_LENGTHS = Map.of(\"61\", 9, \"1\", 10)` \u2014 the one canonical country table (its keys are the recognized dialling codes).\n- `countryCode(String digits)` \u2014 the shared \"longest configured dialling code that prefixes these digits\" selection, returning `Optional<String>`.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 removed the private `COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTHS` literal; `validateNationalNumberLength` now calls `E164PhoneNumbers.countryCode(...)` and reads the required length from `E164PhoneNumbers.NATIONAL_LENGTHS`. Fixed the stale `{@link #COUNTRY_NATIONAL_LENGTHS}` javadoc reference and added the import. (`Comparator`/`Map` are still used elsewhere, so their imports stayed.)\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `toTelephoneDisplay` now derives the country code via `E164PhoneNumbers.countryCode(...)` instead of its own `TELEPHONE_COUNTRY_CODES` list, which I deleted along with the now-unused `Comparator` import. The display-specific behaviour is preserved: the `.filter(c -> digits.length() > c.length())` guard keeps a code from consuming the whole number, and the two-digit fallback for unrecognized codes stays in the mapper (it's a formatting concern, not a validation fact).\n\nNow adding a country to `NATIONAL_LENGTHS` updates both validation and display at once \u2014 no hand-syncing. Behaviour is unchanged: `\"+61412345678\"` \u2192 `\"+61 412 345 678\"`, and the full suite passes.",
